[Music]  at least 10 percent of the people who've  had covet in the u.s about 2 million of  our fellow americans are considered  so-called long-haulers meaning they  still have symptoms of the virus long  after getting the virus itself  well researchers are now moving to start  a clinical trial  for experimental drugs that could help  these people but so far  none have been approved and there are  not any treatments our doctor tara  narula takes us now inside some of the  rehab clinics  or long haulers that are popping up all  across the country and she's going to  show us  how critical their situation really is  when we first met kevin raffel back in  april we love you  he had just woken up from a medically  induced coma because of covid  complications  today he's back home but hasn't fully  recovered there's a lot of memory loss  being out of breath a lot of fatigue a  lot of joint pain  things like that you wonder if you're  going to get everything back but  we don't know yet he falls in the  estimated 10 percent of covid patients  who are long-haulers  survivors who months after being  infected with the virus  are still experiencing debilitating and  often crippling symptoms  there are days when you try to get up  your body just  won't cooperate your limbs won't move  your brain is too dizzy for you to even  sit up  let alone stand up 25 year old michelle  sogi went from a fit and healthy athlete  to living with chronic muscle pain and  fatigue  shortness of breath and brain fog i  actually could no longer live by myself  anymore the symptoms were so  debilitating i couldn't  get up i couldn't shower she's now a  patient at uc davis's post covid 19  clinic  one of several new specialty care  clinics emerging across the country  specifically dedicated to treating  long-hauler patients  what happens at the post covid clinic  that you attend  yeah so initially you get seen by  whoever is the  um most appropriate person to see you  for your prominent issues so for me  that was breathing because coconut is so  unknown it's affecting people in so many  different ways  it is a multi-step multi-disciplinary  process  dr margot gage an epidemiologist  professor and mom of two  has a team of specialists working with  her on her various issues  i have skin rashes i have vision  issues i have ringing in my ears i'm on  an inhaler  and have a sort of asthma even though  i'm not  an asthmatic person she also describes  headaches  stronger than migraines and says on bad  days is bedridden  what are the doctors saying to you now  about your condition that  they are readily admitting that i have  post-covered  symptoms we have our own mode of  treatment and plan that we carry out so  it's very a one-to-one  type of treatment modality what i'm  undergoing  dr virginia witt works at an outpatient  post-covet care facility in new jersey  and says the need for these clinics is  dire we have patients who have  obviously lung damage scarring they're  short of breath  they have no exercise tolerance some of  them even need lung transplants for the  long haul  some patients are on dialysis we have  people with heart problems  no matter how good how better i get  i will never be the same person that i  was  narcisso rodriguez is one of dr witt's  patients  being treated for damage to his lungs  which has impacted his ability to work  before i used to work you know 14 16  hours a day  and it didn't bother me but now i can  one of the biggest issues seen at these  clinics is chronic fatigue  and brain fog alexandra hackett says her  life radically changed  seemingly overnight from breathlessness  to cognitive issues  i started to recognize that i've been  taking an immense amount of  concentration  to get through my day it's an itch you  can't scratch  mentally once a week she sees a  neurotherapist  at mcgee rehabilitation in philadelphia  you went from somebody who was  totally healthy to someone who now has a  cardiologist a pulmonologist  is doing neuro rehab your whole life has  changed  that's why i want to share my story  because i know that there are people out  there  long haulers who are suffering with  symptoms who are  far more debilitating keep going you  don't want  to have your future reduced to nothing  because of an illness a lot of people do  survive  a lot of people do but there are also a  host of people  who survive but are no longer the same  wow dr narula joins us now dr narula it  was great to hear those case studies but  i'm interested what are the big themes  you've heard as you interview these  so-called long haulers  well one of the big things tony is the  frustration of not being believed is  presenting to your doctor with all these  complaints and being told it's in your  head go see a psychiatrist  another centers around the anxiety of  just not knowing are these symptoms  going to get better when are they going  to get better how long is this going to  go on  and then the third big theme really  centered around suddenly becoming going  from somebody who was a caregiver to  somebody who had to be cared for  and what burden that may be placing on  their family members  but many of them spoke about the support  they're getting from these online social  media type groups and so that's been  really great for them  and dr narula i would be remiss if i let  you go without pointing out that you are  now among the  vaccinated americans at least one dose  last week you got it we have a picture  of it there  how are you feeling i feel good i had  some arm pain a little fever and chills  that night but  it was really i was so surprised at how  emotional i got i started  really tearing up at it you know it just  um all the months of being a doctor and  caring for patients myself and being  worried that i was gonna bring it home  to my family  hearing the stories of my friends and  colleagues and then being a journalist  and covering this  it was just a very emotional moment one  where  i felt a release of fear hope and just  tremendous gratitude for science no it's  emotional for us too i mean i was  hopeful when the authorizations came  through but then  frustrated as we see the slow pace of  rollout glad you got it  i'm very excited to see more and more  americans get it as well dr gorilla  thank you very much

